```

## 2. Escape the AI category before it reaches an inline onclick

`showAIBadge` builds an `onclick` attribute from the API response:

```js
<button onclick="acceptAI('${data.category}')">
```

A malformed or hostile classifier response breaks out of the quotes.
Validate against the known set instead — it also stops a bad response
from putting an invalid value into the select.

```js
const VALID = ['road', 'water', 'electric', 'sanitation', 'other'];

// in showAIBadge, before building the HTML:
if (!VALID.includes(data.category)) { showAIBadge('error'); return; }
```

Apply the same check in `runClassify` and the image handler before
`catEl.value = data.category`.

## Known behaviour worth deciding on

Classification runs in the browser and the client submits whatever ends up
in the `<select>`. Nothing stops a user classifying as `water` and
submitting `road`. Not a security hole — the category is not a privilege —
but it means the AI classifier does not actually control routing.

If you want the classifier to be real, call it server-side in the POST
handler in `routes/issues.js` and treat the client value as a hint:

```js
const { category, confidence } = await classify(issueData.title, issueData.description);
issueData.category = category;
issueData.aiTagged = true;
issueData.aiConf   = confidence;
```

